package codegen

// Package codegen is the contract between the compiler's front end and
// its code generation backend, a port of the Go compiler's package
// codegen (backend.go): the backends the driver may name, the
// optimization levels a build may run at, and Refuse, which says whether
// a program may be lowered at all; and of the C backend, Go's
// codegen/c, kept in this package while it is the one backend: the
// header, tack.h (header.tck, Go's header.go), with the names it may not
// give (reserved.tck, Go's reserved.go and any.go's anyIDs and
// viewAnyID), and the lowering of a program to one C translation unit,
// ported in stages, of which the core, E1, values, E2, owned values and
// containers, E3, and generics and functions, E4, are here: the whole of
// it.
//
// generate refuses what Go's Refuse refuses, then rejects, with
// header_diags, a program whose types, fields, results structs or
// extern funcs tack.h could not name, as Go's generate does before it
// lowers anything, and then lowers the program (lower, gen.tck), as Go's
// generate does: the globals noted, the string pool filled, every
// declaration lowered before anything is written, and then the file in
// C's order of declaration, the preamble, the types, the pooled strings
// and the builtins' prototypes, the globals, the results structs, the
// prototypes, the functions and C's main. header renders tack.h as Go's
// Header does, which the driver's --port-header writes with nothing
// lowered and --emit-header writes, once the program is lowered, into
// the package directories, as Go's writeHeaders does.
//
// # The files
//
// Each file ports a part of Go's codegen/c, its header naming which:
//
//   - gen.tck: the generator's state and its top level, Go's gen.go from
//     generate to emitEntryPoint, with the globals, the constant pool,
//     the results structs, the prototypes, the signatures and a
//     function's body;
//   - pool.tck: the string pool, Go's collectStrings and its helpers,
//     and string.go;
//   - unused.tck: what no other function names, Go's unused.go and
//     unusedParams;
//   - walks.tck: the walks of the program and of a body the core asks,
//     usesType, narrowsFloats, mutated, callsFunc, moves and
//     caseVarsRead;
//   - types.tck: the walk of the program's types, Go's emitTypes, with
//     each type's definition after its parts' and the equality helpers;
//   - stmt.tck: the statements, from generateBlock to generateVar;
//   - expr.tck: the expressions, a machine of routines (see below);
//   - order.tck: the order of evaluation, Go's order.go but its spills;
//   - names.tck: the C names and literals, mangling among them, and
//     names.go;
//   - struct.tck, array.tck, union.tck, view.tck: E2's values, Go's
//     struct.go, array.go, union.go and view.go, with slice.go's and
//     any.go's helpers in view.tck: the C structs, the equality
//     helpers, computed constants and globals too deep for an
//     initializer, the addresses helpers read through, and a switch on a
//     union;
//   - values.tck: the routines of the expression machine for E2's
//     values, a composite literal, a field, a union built, a view
//     argument and a slice;
//   - switch.tck: the switches, Go's switch.go;
//   - multi.tck: several values and the checked forms, Go's gen.go from
//     generateMultiVar to bounds;
//   - divide.tck: the division and shift helpers;
//   - swap.tck: swap;
//   - defer.tck: E3's defers and cleanup chains, Go's defer.go, with
//     gen.go's block and generateRegion: a block's frees and defers,
//     the routes of return, break, continue and ? through them, and a
//     function's frame, its ret and next variables;
//   - list.tck: lists, Go's list.go, with drop.go and replace.go: make,
//     append, reserve, pop, remove, replace and drop, the checked forms,
//     a move, the frees of an owned value and the free helpers, union.go's
//     among them;
//   - maps.tck, arena.tck, pools.tck: maps, arenas and pools, Go's
//     maps.go, arena.go and pool.go, their C structs, their helpers and
//     the hash helpers, their builtins, lookups and ranges;
//   - question.tck: the ?, Go's question.go, and the guard of a
//     construct's owned operands;
//   - generic.tck: generic functions, Go's generic.go: the instances,
//     each lowered under its binding, the target of a call, an interface
//     method's stand-in bound to its struct's or union's method, an
//     instance's C name, and the bindings, counted so that what owned
//     keeps is asked only with none in force;
//   - closure.tck: closures and scope parameters, Go's closure.go: a
//     closure's value, its environment and captures, the closures a body
//     holds lowered once it is, the adapters a plain function value
//     passed to a scope parameter is called through, and a call through
//     a scope parameter;
//   - funcvalue.tck: function values, Go's funcvalue.go: the function
//     types' C types, a function named as a value, the thunks of
//     functions of several results, and a call through a value;
//   - fields.tck: fields loops, Go's fields.go, unrolled a block a field
//     under the field's type, v.[f], f.name and f.index;
//   - makedata.tck: make of a Data type, Go's makedata.go.
//
// # The stages
//
// The backend is ported in stages, as tackc's porttest stages its cases
// by what Go's backend runs on them: E1, the core; E2, values, structs,
// arrays, unions, switches, slices, views, any, swap, division and
// shifts, several values and the checked forms; E3, owned values and
// containers, lists, maps, arenas, pools, defers, ?, drop, replace, the
// free helpers and regions; and E4, generics and functions, instances,
// switches on type parameters, closures, scope parameters, function
// values, fields loops, make of Data and calls through a value. All four
// are here. Everything is lowered into buffers before anything is
// written, as Go's generate does, so a program declined at any point
// writes nothing, no partial C, and the driver holds the warnings back
// until the C or tack.h is out: no number used by a lowering cut short
// shows anywhere. The port answers not_ported, which the driver reports
// as not implemented, exit status 3, where Go reports an internal error
// (see below). Where the C Go writes for an accepted program is a Go bug,
// the port writes C that is right instead, each named in a comment, and
// tackc's porttest lists the case as a known divergence from Go: the
// zero value of a type parameter that stands for a string, an Allocator,
// an any or an error, which Go writes as 0 (see append_zero_value); the
// zero value of a string where Go's walk pooled no empty string, whose
// label Go leaves empty, which the port pools after the walk (see
// append_empty_string); the empty string a pool[string]'s take helper
// leaves in its slot, which Go names before the pool declares it (see
// emit_pools); and has written as a statement, which Go lowers to
// a call of no C function, and which is evaluated and its value dropped,
// as len's is (see generate_expr_stmt). The header reports an extern
// func's C name that clashes with a type's or that tack.h reserves at
// the C name, and names the package of a struct whose name is a C
// keyword, claiming its name, where Go does neither (see header.tck).
// Once every body is lowered the pool is closed, and a zero value that
// would need the empty string pooled then is declined.
//
// # Numbering
//
// C names carry numbers Go hands out as it lowers: temporaries, tack_
// and a prefix and the count of temporaries named so far in the file,
// and pooled strings, tack_str and their place in the pool. Every
// temporary is named where Go names it, in Go's order, the ones a render
// later thrown away named among them: a spill scope rendered again, deep
// (see expr.tck), keeps the counter where the first render left it, as
// Go's errRetry does, so the numbers it used stay used. The pool fills
// in the order of Go's walk; Go finds a string in it by scanning, and
// here a map finds it, which gives the same label. The type table adds
// a type while the program is lowered only where Go's lowering asks it
// for one: a binding, E4's, and the view of a list's or an arena's
// elements a spread or an arena's span helper asks for, and the list of
// an arena's elements, each asked in Go's order. Labels, tack_defer and
// a number, are counted apart from the temporaries, as Go counts them,
// and so are the routes of a frame's exits. A closure is numbered,
// tack_closureN, in the order its value is rendered, a render thrown
// away and rendered again included, as Go's are, and its body, lowered
// once the body holding it is, is lowered even then; a thunk and an
// adapter are written once for each function and function type, in the
// order first met. Every binding is made where Go makes one, an
// instance's, a fields loop's field's, a switch on a type parameter's
// case's and a closure's again where it was met, and every query of the
// table asked in Go's order, the ones whose answers Go ignores among
// them, so the types a binding adds get Go's IDs.
//
// # Stack discipline
//
// Go renders an expression by recursion, a frame a level, and a chain
// of operators, of && among them, is as long as its source makes it, so
// the expressions are rendered by a machine with a stack of its own
// (expr.tck): each of Go's functions on the way, expr, exprSteps,
// spilled, exprNode, binaryExpr, otherScope, unaryExpr, callValue,
// callExpr and the rest, is a routine of it, whose steps do what Go's
// function does between two of its calls, in Go's order. Every walk of
// the tree keeps a stack of its own (see push_children in pool.tck), and
// so does every walk of the types, which Go makes by recursion a frame a
// level of a type's parts, and a chain of structs, each holding the one
// before, is as long as its source makes it: the definitions, the
// equality helpers and the types compared (types.tck), the free helpers,
// a value's frees and whether a type owns (list.tck, gen.tck), the hash
// helpers (maps.tck), and a computed constant's rendering and pooling
// (struct.tck, pool.tck). Every routine of the expression machine E3
// adds, a ? among them, is a routine like the core's, so a ? in an
// argument of a call in a ? costs no frame a level either.
// The instances of generic functions are lowered in a loop, however
// many instantiate further ones, and so are the closures a body holds,
// those of the closures in them included, each its own frame once the
// body around it is done, however deeply they nest; make of a Data type
// walks the struct it makes from a stack of its own, and an instance's
// name, its type arguments as deep as they are, is written by the
// table's own walk (see package types). A fields loop and a switch on a
// type parameter lower each block they hold as any block in a statement
// is.
// Statements nest in blocks, and blocks in statements, as deeply as the
// parser lets them, a few frames a level, each kept small; a chain of
// else ifs and a run of statements are walked in a loop, so neither
// costs a frame a link. Go's commaList recurses as deep as the
// logarithm of the number of steps it joins, and so does its port.
//
// # Lines
//
// A lowered body is a list of Lines, Go's []string: each a run of one
// buffer, Gen.text, that every line is written to, or one of the two
// markers Go's indent puts around a nested block, which emit_func turns
// into indentation as it writes the lines, as Go's emitFunc does, so a
// line is never rebuilt for each level around it. What noteNames records
// Go records of every identifier of every body; only names that start
// tack_ are ever asked about, so only those are recorded here.
//
// It takes a checked program as the type checker hands it over, a
// typeck.Typed (see package typeck): the program, its files, its type
// table and every annotation Go's checker writes on the tree that Go's
// backend reads. The type table comes as a types.Bound, which answers
// every query under the binding in force, as Go's Table does, and hides
// the Table's own queries, which answer as if nothing were bound: the
// backend binds a generic function's type parameters while it lowers an
// instance, and C names carry the IDs of the types a binding adds, so
// no query it makes may skip the binding, and through a Bound none can.
//
// Go registers its backends from init into a map, which Tack has no
// place for: there is one backend, c, so the registry is the list
// backend_names, in Go's sorted order, which has_backend searches, as
// Go's Get does, and which --backends writes, one a line, as Go's Names
// gives them. Go's Register, which panics on a name registered twice,
// has nothing to check here. A Typed exists only for a program the type
// checker has checked, so of Go's Refuse's refusals only the one for a
// program with errors can be made here; the others, for no program and
// for one never checked, cannot happen.
//
// Where Go's backend reports an internal error whose message names a Go
// type with %T, an unknown declaration, statement or expression, the
// port declines instead. The testing.Site arguments a call passes, which
// Go's checker splices among its arguments, are put among them as nodes
// of the tree before anything is lowered (see Typed.splice_sites), so
// every walk and every rendering meets them where Go's does.
//
// The commands a build runs, Go's CompileCmd, CompileStdCmd, BuildCmd
// and VersionCmd, come with the driver's builds, which the port does not
// do yet.
